#
# holt — gap-language definitions for universal-ctags.
#
# MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. universal-ctags 6.2.1 ships 164 languages, but `ctags --print-language`
# returns NONE for these:
#
#   Swift · Scala · Dart · Groovy · Solidity · Zig · Nim · Crystal · F# · Prolog
#   Dockerfile · GraphQL
#
# That is a real hole for a tool claiming to work on any project: Swift and Dart are most of
# mobile, Scala and Groovy are a large share of the JVM world, and Solidity/Zig/Nim/Crystal are
# where a lot of new code is being written. A symbol layer blind to those languages silently
# reports "no duplicates, no collisions" on entire ecosystems — the exact false-negative this
# tool exists to prevent.
#
# WHY optlib RATHER THAN A SECOND EXTRACTOR:
#   ctags' own parser-definition language (--langdef / --regex-<LANG>) extends ctags itself, so
#   there is ONE symbol pipeline with one output format and one set of downstream semantics.
#   The alternative — a parallel regex path in JavaScript for "the languages ctags lacks" — means
#   two code paths that drift, two sets of kind names, and two places to fix a bug.
#
#   These are regex parsers, so they are less precise than ctags' hand-written C parsers. They
#   are tuned to over-extract rather than under-extract: a spurious symbol produces a finding a
#   human dismisses, while a missed symbol produces silence that reads as "nothing there".
#
# Users can extend this: point HOLT_CTAGS_OPTIONS at your own .ctags file and it is loaded
# after this one, so your definitions win.
#

### Adjustments to ctags' own parsers ##########################################
#
# Function prototypes are OFF by default in the C/C++ parsers, because a prototype is not a
# definition. For holt that default is wrong: two agents each declaring `void handle();` in the
# same header is precisely the collision we exist to report, and with prototypes disabled it is
# invisible. Measured: `void cppMethod();` produced no tag at all until this was enabled.
--kinds-C=+p
--kinds-C++=+p

# NOTE ON AMBIGUOUS EXTENSIONS (.fs, .m, .h, .pl, .r, .v, .t …):
# There is deliberately NO extension remapping here. An earlier revision hardcoded `.fs` to F#
# by taking it away from Forth, which is a guess dressed up as a fix — it is simply wrong for
# every Forth repository, and the same problem recurs for .m (Objective-C vs MATLAB vs Mercury),
# .h (C vs C++ vs Objective-C), .pl (Perl vs Prolog) and a dozen more.
#
# holt resolves these by CONTENT using enry (github.com/go-enry/go-enry) — the Go port of
# GitHub Linguist, with Linguist's own Bayesian classifier trained on Linguist's samples.
# Verified: F#-content .fs -> F#, Forth-content .fs -> Forth, Objective-C .m -> Objective-C,
# MATLAB .m -> MATLAB. See resolveAmbiguous() in src/symbols.mjs.
#
# When enry is not installed, holt falls back to ctags' own extension mapping and REPORTS the
# degradation rather than silently guessing.


